  "kind": "statement",
  "title": "Finiteness of the morphism from two linear forms",
  "statement": "In the setup of the morphism\n$\\pi_{l_0,l_1}$, that morphism is\nfinite.",
  "source": {
    "document": "literature/blueprint/master/blueprint/sec-picard-framework/sk-thm-picard-coherence/lem-pc-linear-pair-morphism-finite.md",
    "place": "node lem-pc-linear-pair-morphism-finite, order 653"
  },
  "stmt_deps": [],
  "proof": [
    {
      "text": "Choose $N$ as in [lem-pc-linear-pair-graded-finite].\nEvery $S_d$ is finite-dimensional: it is spanned by the finitely\nmany degree-$d$ monomials in the generators of\n[def-pc-standard-graded-algebra].\nTake the union of bases of $S_0,\\ldots,S_{N-1}$, and denote\nits elements by $g_1,\\ldots,g_r$ with degrees $d_j$.\nInduction on $n$, using $S_n=l_0S_{n-1}+l_1S_{n-1}$ for\n$n\\ge N$, expresses each $s\\in S_n$ as\n\n$$\ns=\\sum_{j=1}^r F_j(l_0,l_1)g_j,\n$$\n\nwhere each $F_j$ is homogeneous of degree $n-d_j$ (and is zero\nif that degree is negative). This proves finite generation of\n$S$ as a module over the image of $k[T_0,T_1]\\to S$,\n$T_i\\mapsto l_i$; injectivity of this ring map is unnecessary.",
      "uses": [
        "lem-pc-linear-pair-graded-finite",
        "def-pc-standard-graded-algebra"
      ],
      "elementary": false
    },
    {
      "text": "The inverse image of $D_+(T_i)\\subset\\mathbf P^1_k$ is exactly\n$D_+(l_i)$ by [def-pc-linear-pair-morphism] (the morphism is the\none glued in [lem-pc-linear-pair-morphism-gluing]). It is affine\nwith coordinate ring $S_{(l_i)}$ by [def-pc-proj].\nFor $i=0$, divide the displayed homogeneous expression by $l_0^n$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{s}{l_0^n}\n=\\sum_j F_j(1,l_1/l_0)\\frac{g_j}{l_0^{d_j}}.\n$$",
      "uses": [
        "def-pc-linear-pair-morphism",
        "lem-pc-linear-pair-morphism-gluing",
        "def-pc-proj"
      ],
      "elementary": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Thus the finitely many $g_j/l_0^{d_j}$ generate $S_{(l_0)}$\nas a module over $k[t]$, with $t$ acting by $l_1/l_0$.\nThe identical computation with $l_1$ proves finite generation\nover $k[t^{-1}]$ on the other chart.\nThe affine-open definition of a finite morphism in\n[def-pc-morphism-conventions] now proves the assertion.",
      "uses": [
        "def-pc-morphism-conventions"
      ],
      "elementary": false
    }
  ],
